<header><p class="st-kicker">chiefdom · West Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Duala kingdoms</h1><p class="st-meta">1650 CE – 1884 CE (approx.) · capital Douala</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Trading chiefdoms of the Duala people on the Wouri estuary, whose kings Bell and Akwa controlled commerce with Europeans and signed the treaty that created German Kamerun.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duala-people">Duala</a> settled on the Wouri estuary, probably in the 17th century, and by the late 1600s were dealing with European ships that called at the river. Their settlements grew into rival lineage chiefdoms, chief among them Bell (Bonanjo) and Akwa, with Deido and Bonaberi as smaller units. Leaders styled themselves kings in dealings with European traders.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Power rested on middleman trade: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duala-people">Duala</a> bought slaves, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a> and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palm-oil">palm oil</a> and palm kernels from inland peoples and sold them to British, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> and German merchants, refusing outsiders direct access to the interior. Kings ruled with lineage heads and the ngondo assembly. British missionaries, notably <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfred-saker">Alfred Saker</a> from 1845, introduced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baptists">Baptist</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> and a written Duala language.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>On 12 July 1884 King Bell and King Akwa signed a treaty with German agents, and the estuary became the core of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kamerun">Kamerun</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/douala">Douala</a> grew into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cameroon">Cameroon's</a> main port, and Bell and Akwa families remained prominent; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rudolf-duala-manga-bell">Rudolf Duala Manga Bell</a> was executed by the Germans in 1914.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="chiefdom, 1650 CE – 1884 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><ellipse cx="44" cy="56" rx="16" ry="24"/><path d="M44 34 V78"/><path d="M68 20 V84"/><path d="M68 20 L63 30 H73 Z"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">chiefdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">West Africa</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:92.54%;width:4.66%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1650 CE – 1884 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>chiefdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Douala</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Ndumbe Lobe Bell, Dika Mpondo Akwa</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Duala traditional religion, later Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Duala</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/cameroon">Cameroon</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Duala chiefdoms, Kingdom of Douala, Bell and Akwa kingdoms</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1767&amp;focus=duala-kingdoms" aria-label="Duala kingdoms on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4949 2026 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Duala kingdoms lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5269 2282L5256 2278"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5256 2278h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5269 2282h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5269 2282h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Douala. <a href="/atlas?year=1767&amp;focus=duala-kingdoms">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

